# Guards that hold two sets to each other without calling guard_hold.
#
# guard_hold in tests/guard-paths.sh is the one way a guard compares a
# derived set against a register: both directions, both sorted the same
# way, the items shown through the caller's own formatter if it has one.
# A guard that writes the pair of comm invocations itself has copied
# that helper, and a copy drifts -- one direction gets edited and the
# other does not, leaving a check that is blind to whatever is absent
# from the list it started from, which is exactly the state a newly
# added member is in.
#
# So writing the pair by hand is refused unless the guard is named here
# with a reason. Only the pair is refused: a single comm subtracting one
# set from another to build a third is an ordinary set operation, and
# several guards need it.
#
# THIS FILE IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE STATE TO KEEP IT IN. Every guard
# that holds two sets to each other calls the helper. A name added here
# is a guard that has stopped doing so, and it owes a reason and what
# converting it would cost.
#
# An entry naming a guard that has since adopted the helper, or a guard
# that is no longer in tests/, is itself a failure -- an exemption that
# outlives what it excused goes on to excuse whatever is written there
# next.
#
#   <script>   <reason>

